{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1092", "sample_idx": 45, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "**A Whirlwind Adventure in Japan: Cherry Blossoms and Neon Lights**\n\nFrom the moment I stepped off the plane in Tokyo, Japan felt like a dream. The first stop was **Shibuya Crossing**, where a sea of pedestrians moved with effortless grace beneath towering digital billboards. The energy was electric—taxis honked, rickshaws glided past, and somewhere between the crowds, I spotted a tiny *ishiyaki* stall serving skewered fish over charcoal.\n\nThe next morning, I took the Shinkansen (bullet train) to Kyoto, where ancient temples met modern elegance. At **Fushimi Inari Shrine**, thousands of vermilion torii gates stretched up the mountainside, their pathways winding like a labyrinth. I climbed until the shrine vanished below, leaving only the distant chatter of tourists and the rustle of wind through paper lanterns.\n\nSpring had painted Kyoto in soft pinks—**Maruyama Park** bloomed with cherry blossoms, their petals drifting onto the stone paths. I shared a matcha latte at a tiny café, the bittersweet powder clinging to my lips as I watched geisha glide past in the distance.\n\nIn Osaka, I lost myself in **Dotonbori**, where neon signs flickered in hues of blue and pink. Street food stalls offered takoyaki (octopus balls) and okonomiyaki (savory pancakes), my hands sticky with sauce and crumbs. At night, the canal reflected the glow of Ferris wheels and glowing characters, turning the city into a living postcard.\n\nThe trip ended too soon, but Japan’s contrasts—its temples and arcades, silence and chaos—left an unforgettable mark. If you ever go, pack light, say *\"arigatou\"* often, and be ready for magic around every corner.", "logprobs": [{"token": "**", "logprob": -0.06550939381122589}, {"token": "A", "logprob": -0.27843376994132996}, {"token": " Wh", "logprob": -0.037113383412361145}, {"token": "irl", "logprob": -0.018043426796793938}